<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akkar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akkar is the northernmost region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, a fertile coastal plain and hill country on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, now a governorate. The plain lies along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> Gap, the route from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast to the Orontes valley, and was controlled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> by Sumur, probably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-kazel">Tell Kazel</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akkar.jpg" alt="Akkar" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FunkMonk · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:3133_(20).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Amarna age, around 1350 BCE, the Lebanese coastal city-states fought for dominance as Egypt declined: the <b>Akkar</b> Plain rebelled with Hittite support to become the kingdom of Amurru, the Mitanni Empire that had dominated Syria fell apart under the campaigns of Suppiluliuma I of Hatti, and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Sumur, probably Tell Kazel near the Lebanese border, controlled the <b>Akkar</b> plain and the gap leading inland to the Orontes.</blockquote><a href="/state/simyra">Simyra</a></li></ul></section>
